---
// "How this works" for the practice engine (M6.3 text, placed by M6.4). Beginner level, honest about
// being a set of heuristics. Sources: S-037 (knowledge tracing), S-038 (SM-2), S-039 (Thompson sampling).
import Badge from '../ui/Badge.astro';
interface Props {
  headingLevel?: 2 | 3;
}
const { headingLevel = 2 } = Astro.props;
const H = `h${headingLevel}` as 'h2' | 'h3';
---

<section class="how-this-works" aria-labelledby="how-this-works">
  <H id="how-this-works">
    How this works <Badge variant="heuristic" />
  </H>
  <p>
    The practice page chooses your next question with three simple models. They are rules of thumb with fixed
    settings, not a trained artificial intelligence, and they can be wrong about you. You can always pick a
    topic yourself instead.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>How well you know each idea.</strong> Every concept keeps a number between 0 and 1: the chance
      that you know it. A right answer raises it, a wrong one lowers it, and a little is added each time you
      practise because practice teaches. This is called knowledge tracing (
      <a href="/references/#S-037">Corbett and Anderson, 1994</a>). We call a concept mastered at 0.95.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>When to see a question again.</strong> After a good answer the next review is 1 day later, then
      6 days, then longer and longer gaps. A wrong answer brings the question back tomorrow. This is the SM-2
      spacing rule (<a href="/references/#S-038">Wozniak, 1990</a>), used by many flashcard apps.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What kind of item to give you.</strong> There are a few kinds: a review that is due, an easier
      new question, a question at about your level, a harder one, a hint, or a topic to read. The page keeps a
      running score of how well each kind has helped you and tries the promising ones more often, while still
      trying the others now and then. This is Thompson sampling (
      <a href="/references/#S-039">Thompson, 1933</a>).
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>Some rules are fixed and never learned:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Reviews that are more than 3 days overdue come first.</li>
    <li>
      You are not given questions on an idea whose foundations all look shaky, unless nothing else is left.
    </li>
    <li>After 3 wrong answers in a row you are offered a hint or an easier question.</li>
    <li>
      There are no timers. The time you take is used only to tune the choice of item; it is never shown or
      scored.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Everything stays in this browser on this device. Nothing is sent anywhere unless you switch on progress
    sync and sign in.
  </p>
</section>
